[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'AI is showing up in every industry right now, but real estate agents stand a lot to gain here. And if you haven\'t started, you\'re missing out. Or perhaps you\'ve dipped your toes in and you\'re nowhere near maximizing what the stuff can actually do. So, how does an average'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'agent actually use AI to win more listings, close more sales, and get their time back? In this video, I\'m showing you exactly what I tell my real estate friends to do in this area of AI, &gt;&gt; [music]'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; coming up. Now, we actually ran our own analysis on this. We went and looked at what agents are making with AI right now. Thousands of videos, hundreds of agents, and a few things stood out. Three quarters of'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'realtors are on social media, but fewer than half are using AI to actually make the content. And most agents who quit posting quit at around the six-week mark. They look at 200 views, decide video doesn\'t work in their market, and'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they stop. And that\'s the part that gets me. They\'re not wrong about the results. They\'re wrong about the reason. Because the agent who stick with it and actually use AI, most of them save at least an hour a week, and a third save more than'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'four. So, there\'s a gap, and it\'s a big one. And now, I\'m not a real estate agent. I make videos for a living. But I spent months watching agents try to do both, sell homes and be a content creator at the same time. So, here are'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'six core moves I\'d run in my workflow if I had a license and an area I was farming for leads. And move three is one I\'d pay close attention to if you really want to save yourself some hours. Now, like I said, I\'m not an agent. So,'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I had a chat with a close friend of mine to find out what a typical week actually looks like. And yes, I\'m talking about my good friend Claude. Don\'t judge me. He\'s a great listener. So, what I did here was ask the question, what does a'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'typical week look like for a real estate agent, and what do they struggle with most when it comes to marketing themselves and getting listings? What I got back was that mornings are prospecting, mid-days are appointments and showings, and afternoons are the'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'admin swamp. And the evenings and weekends, more showings because that\'s when clients are actually free. And then there\'s this line, which I think is the best summary of the problem. Marketing is a block that gets eaten. It\'s never urgent, so it loses every single'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'collision with every client who needs something right now. Then it flagged something I did not expect, camera anxiety. Agents avoid making content entirely because being visible online feels uncomfortable. And for a'

[02:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'profession built entirely on personal brand, that is a brutal bottleneck. But the big one is the very first thing it said, marketing consistency, not marketing ideas. And read that carefully because it\'s the opposite of what'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everybody assumes. It\'s not that agents are out of ideas, it\'s that the process feels messy. They don\'t know what to say, what to shoot, or how to stop a session from dragging on for hours. And that\'s not an inspiration problem, that\'s a systems problem. And a systems'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'problem has a completely different fix. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; So that\'s where the real bottleneck is, not the ideas or the systems. And you don\'t need anything fancy for [music] this. You can use Claude, ChatGPT, or'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Gemini, whatever you already pay for. It genuinely does not matter. Watch what happens when you ask the right questions. [music] So to follow up, I gave it some information here. I said, "I\'m a real estate agent in Austin,'

[03:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Texas. Build me a weekly content plan for the next month. Tell me the specific types of videos I should be posting, how often, and what job each one does for my business." Okay, great. So look what comes back. Five content pillars, market'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'updates, hyper local, objection education, proof of work, [music] and face or POV. Four posts a week plus a long-form a month. And it explains the job each one'

[03:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'does, which is what I care about most. Now, market updates win listing appointments because the sellers [music] hire the agent who sounds like they know their numbers. The proof of work is seller recruitment because sellers watching that video are deciding whether'

[03:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you can market their house. Now, is any of this groundbreaking? Not really. It\'s a solid, sensible plan, but it\'s completely correct, and you got it in about 11 seconds. [music] But, here\'s what also got my attention. Three of'

[03:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'those four pillars already exist as formats in HeyGen. Market update, same [music] name, it\'s actually the same thing. Now, the proof of work, that\'s a home tour. The face and POV, that\'s [music] Meet the Agent. And that\'s not a'

[04:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'coincidence. These formats exist because they\'re the videos agents were already making, which lines up almost exactly with what we found when we talked to agents ourselves. [music] And then it said the thing that made me want to make this video in the first place. The plan'

[04:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'isn\'t the hard part. The plan is the easy part. Agents don\'t feel that content because they run out of ideas. They fail because the process is messy, and the block gets eaten by a client who needs something right now. So, with AI,'

[04:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we strategize a whole plan in a couple of seconds, and everything from here is just execution. And execution is exactly where AI stops being a novelty and starts being the only reason any of this is sustainable.'

[04:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But, that plan comes with a catch. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Because now you have a content calendar, and executing it means being on camera constantly. [music] Nobody sustains that on top of a real week. And remember, one of the issues'

[04:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'highlighted was camera anxiety. The advice agents are getting is three to five videos a week with their [music] face every single time. For one person also running showings and contracts, that math just doesn\'t work out. So,'

[05:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'guess what? You can build an avatar. You record it once and show up everywhere. Your face stops being a scheduling problem. In HeyGen, that\'s with Avatar 5 or 4. About 15 seconds of phone footage, and [music]'

[05:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re done. But, here\'s the part people skip, and it\'s the part that actually matters. Your avatar inherits your footage, the camera, the lighting, [music] and your energy. If you have a flat delivery in, you get flat avatar out. So, spend the time here. Make sure'

[05:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you have good lighting on your face, a steady camera [music] with eye contact down the lens. Also, ensure you have a plain background, a quiet room, and actual energy. 15 seconds of effort that every video after this one inherits. And'

[05:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'look, I know some of you are skeptical about the whole avatar thing. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; That\'s fair. But think about what your face actually is in this business. It\'s your brand. It\'s the entire [music] thing. Which means a video that looks almost like you is worse than no video'

[06:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at all. Almost costs you trust and it doesn\'t build it. That\'s why realism here isn\'t a speck on a page. It\'s the whole commercial argument. And one honest caveat here, because I\'m not going to pretend this replaces'

[06:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everything, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; go back to that fifth pillar that AI gave us. Face or POV. The note on it was, people prefer humans, not market analysis. That\'s the one I would not hand over to an avatar. Now, four out of the five, absolutely. [music]'

[06:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But keep one where it\'s unmistakably you. Phone, no edit, and a little imperfect. The avatar buys you consistency. [music] That one buys you connection. And knowing the difference is what separates agents who use this well from agents who get found out.'

[06:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Okay, so this is the one I told you to watch for. Because now we\'re actually making something. And this is where those formats come in. There are three of them, and each one is doing a different job.'

[06:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; We have things like Meet the Agent. This is your business card. You make it once and you use it forever. Then there\'s Market Updates. This is the habit video. It\'s weekly. [music] And this one is the one that compounds. And lastly, we have the Hosted Home Tour. There\'s one per listing. These'

[07:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'aren\'t generic templates with a real estate label slapped on them. They\'re built around the videos agents actually make. Now, here\'s the part that actually surprised me. The input [music] isn\'t a script, although it can be. For a market update, you can type a zip code and'

[07:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that\'s it. The research happens for you. For the home tour, you can simply paste in your link and it pulls the photos itself. Which, by the way, answers a question we get a lot. People ask me what tool they should use to scrape'

[07:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'listing photos. Well, you don\'t need one. [music] The link is the scraper. Now, two quick tips that\'ll save you a regenerate. Name the rooms and features that you care about. If you don\'t, [music] it picks for you. And it might not pick the renovated kitchen. So, I encourage'

[07:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you to use it here. And for market [music] updates, a zip code beats a city name every single time. And I want to be straight with you here about the risk here because it\'s a real one. Generic content erodes trust. If you let AI write something that could have come'

[07:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from any agent in the city, people will feel it. So, the format handles the structure and you handle the market. That part doesn\'t get outsourced. And if you want to go completely off format, video agent or avatar shots will build from scratch. It\'s the same avatar with'

[08:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'no card required. Now, once you\'ve got your video down, there\'s another obvious step that most agents never take. [music] And that is with ads. Most agents skip ads. And the reason is always the same. Creative is'

[08:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'expensive and nobody wants to shoot 10 different versions of themselves. That\'s exactly why an AI avatar is perfect for this situation. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; And you can do things even more cinematic using things like avatar shots and generate the ad creative of the'

[08:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'avatar you already created with multiple variations and no reshoots. And here\'s the only thing you really need to know about agent ads. The reason most of them underperform isn\'t the targeting. It\'s that one tired creative ran for 4 months straight.'

[08:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; So, variation is the whole game. And variation just stops being expensive when you use AI avatars. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Now, here\'s the one that decides whether any of this actually survives because a system that needs a good week will fail,'

[08:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] guaranteed. So, you need a system that survives a bad week. And most AI tools can run on a schedule now. So, why not use it? Set a recurring task to draft next week\'s scripts before the week starts. Monday morning, you\'re not'

[09:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'inventing, you\'re editing, which is a huge difference. And I\'ve been using this for a while inside of Chloe to generate different content ideas and scripts before so that I can take a look once I\'ve arrived at my laptop. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[09:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; So, you can point it at your own inputs. The new listings your team mentioned in the Monday meetings, your notes, or your listing sheet. Now, the scripts are written off real inventory, not generic prompts. So, the week looks like this. Monday, [music]'

[09:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'market update goes out, listing goes live, video with the same day. An open house is coming up, and then your host should talk and come before it. Now, let me be honest with you about the timeline. This is not a viral play. Month one looks like nothing for'

[09:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everybody. The agents who win are the ones still posting in month three. And the only reason most people don\'t get there is that the hallway needs a reshoot every single time. And with AI, you can avoid that. [music]'

[10:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And the last move here has nothing to do with content at all. Remember speed to lead from earlier? The average agent takes over 15 hours to respond to an inbound lead, where 5 minutes is where the conversation actually lives. And'

[10:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] Claude put it better than I would have. Content that generates leads you don\'t answer is a very expensive hobby. So, automate that first. [music] So, we have email. Have AI draft every reply. You\'re editing and sending, not'

[10:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'writing from zero. And after showings, that\'s your biggest time sink. It\'s the same story for lead follow-up, CRM notes, and scheduling. [music] Because here\'s a line I\'d put on the poster for this whole video. Content is'

[10:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the part that has to sound like you. Everything else is admin. [music] And admin is what you hand off first. So, that\'s the workflow that I\'d implement with six moves. [music] And you can start with any one of them tomorrow. But if I\'m being honest, here\'s the part that actually matters.'

[10:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You already have the hard part. You know your [music] market, and people actually trust you. The only thing standing between that and a camera was the camera. And if you\'re starting from zero, personally, [music] I\'d start with your market update. That\'s the one. It'

[11:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'takes minutes, and it\'s the one that compounds. So, tell me in the comments, what\'s actually stopping you from posting weekly? Is it the time? Or is it not knowing what to say? I\'m genuinely curious which one it is. But in the meantime, I\'ve got one other real estate'

[11:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'video that\'s worth your time. Go check it out, and I\'ll see you in the next one.'
